WEBVTT

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.860
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:12.600
So now that we know that we have synchronous sequential circuits and that we have sequential

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.600
circuits, we are making a decision and we are going to stick to that in this course

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.680
that we are going to work with the synchronous version of the circuits.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:37.210
So as I said, they have to synchronize with respect this discrete time instances.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:01:01.400
And the synchronization is achieved by clock generator.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.140
So what does clock generator do?

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:11.980
Well, it provides or generates clock cycles, well, sorry, clock pulses.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:25.330
clock pulses are going to make clock cycles.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:30.330
Clock pulses are very often going to be on schematics,

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:37.820
going to be shown either as clock or simply as CLK.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:54.200
So this clock pulse is going to determine

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.200
when the data will be stored

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.040
in synchronous sequential circuit.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.040
So clock period, well clock period is duration

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.320
of clock pulse.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:46.360
So clock pulse determines when data will be stored

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.040
in synchronous sequential circuit.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:56.040
And I stumbled already a couple of times because I'm thinking all of the time about this.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:05.730
So clock signal, this clock pulse, is going to be a periodical signal.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:18.260
And the clock period of this signal is TCLK, time T and CLK is for clock.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:28.180
fyi, that is equal to 1 over fclk, but f stands for frequency, clock frequency.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:37.620
So let's look at the clock cycle. We said it's periodical. My drawing skills are not fabulous,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:49.360
but I'm going to try to be as nice as possible. And we go like this forever and ever and ever,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:04:01.410
or close to that as possible. If you see from this moment to this moment, we have one instance

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:16.450
of clock period. And after that, within each new period of TCLK, the same signal repeats.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:24.070
So periodical signals are the one that repeats always at the same time. Something that I really

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:31.270
didn't fabulously represent here, but, you know, kind of try to abstract it away.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:40.470
I'm going to try to make it a little bit more even. If you see here,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:51.870
here. Approximately half of the time of this entire clock period, clock is equal to 1 and

00:04:51.870 --> 00:05:05.860
other time, other half, clock is equal to 0. So this is called clock that has 50% duty

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:20.330
cycle and the duty cycle is a fraction of a period in which signal is active.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:46.750
When we say active right now we can think about active means equal to 1.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:06:09.510
So here we have 50% duty cycle. Can we have a clock that looks like this where

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.010
we have 10% duty cycle?

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.330
Yep, absolutely, we can have that.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:23.370
We can also have clock that looks like this,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.790
where our duty cycle is 90%.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.790
So we can have all varieties because,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:34.390
so this is 10% and this here from here to here

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:46.660
is 90% of entire, so this is entire clock period.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.100
and it doesn't need to be 10, 90, 50%.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:56.510
It can be any amount in between.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:57.990
Let's wrap up for now.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.950
In the next video, we are going to look

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:03.230
into first element that can store.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:04.950
We are going to look into SR batch.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:05.790
Thank you.

